9. Assumptions
• The database uses the name cpen208.
• The project uses the cpen208 schema for the application tables.
• Student emails are generated from student IDs because the supplied student data does not include email addresses.
• Sample lecturers, courses, teaching assistants, assignments, invoices, and payments demonstrate the required relationships.
• The first-semester fee data provides sample values for testing the outstanding-fees calculation.
10. Verification
The database scripts define all required tables and relationships, populate the sample data, and create the required outstanding-fees function. The seed script also includes a query for checking the JSON output from get_outstanding_fees(). The resulting database backup preserves the implemented database structure and data for reproduction.

11. Conclusion
Project 1 delivers the required PostgreSQL database for the student management system. The design separates students, courses, staff assignments, enrolments, and financial records into related tables. The project also provides sample data, integrity constraints, supporting indexes and a JSON-returning outstanding-fees function. These database features form the foundation for the API and web application developed in Project 2.